# /etc/apivo/<env>/blnk.env — 0600 root:root
#
# The RUNTIME half of the ledger's configuration: what `blnk start` and
# `blnk workers` read, every second of every day. The migration's half is
# blnk-migrate.env, and the difference between the two files is the founder's
# split posture (2026-08-24) made physical.
#
# Read by the Docker daemon as the `blnk` and `blnk-worker` services'
# `env_file` (deploy/hetzner/compose/docker-compose.cashback.yml). Never
# sourced by the reconciler and never in `docker compose config` output, so
# the ledger's database password does not travel through a shell that also
# writes logs — the same rule api.env exists for, applied to the same kind of
# value.
#
# Blnk accepts either a /blnk.json file or environment variables prefixed
# `BLNK_`. The environment form is used because the credentials belong in a
# 0600 file the daemon reads rather than in a JSON document this repository
# would have to template.
#
# BLNK_REDIS_DNS, BLNK_PROJECT_NAME and BLNK_SERVER_PORT are deliberately
# ABSENT: they are container names and constants the compose file chose, not
# an operator's decisions, so they are set there. Adding them here would
# create a second place to change one address.
#
# Written as a template by provision.sh and then never touched again — like
# api.env, and for the same reason: a script that could fill in the DSN would
# be a script that had to be given it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS — the RUNTIME role. `blnk_app`, NOT the owner.
#
# blnk_app has CONNECT on the database, USAGE on the `blnk` schema, and
# SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on its tables. It owns nothing. What it cannot
# do is the point:
#
#   CREATE SCHEMA          it cannot give itself a second home
#   DDL inside blnk        a compromised ledger process cannot drop or
#                          reshape the tables holding members' balances
#   anything in public     Apivo's tables are legal evidence, and the ledger
#                          has no business seeing them
#
# The role is created ONCE, by an operator, with the recipe in
# scripts/spikes/ledger_schema/bootstrap.sql — run as the database owner
# against the environment's own database:
#
#   psql "$DATABASE_URL" -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 #       -v blnk_password="$(openssl rand -base64 32)" #       -f scripts/spikes/ledger_schema/bootstrap.sql
#
# That file is the production recipe, not a fixture, and it refuses to run
# without a password rather than shipping one. Spike S1 proves the four
# refusals above against a real Postgres, with Postgres's own log as the
# evidence.
#
#   qa       the Postgres container on this host, which serves TLS with a
#            self-signed certificate provision.sh generates:
#
#            postgres://blnk_app:<password>@postgres:5432/apivo?sslmode=require
#
#   staging  the same Supabase EU project the api uses, with blnk_app created
#   prod     in it by the recipe above:
#
#            postgres://blnk_app:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres?sslmode=verify-full
#
# No `search_path` parameter is needed: bootstrap.sql sets it on the role
# itself (`ALTER ROLE blnk_app SET search_path = blnk`), so even a session
# that skipped Blnk's own `SET search_path TO blnk` resolves inside the
# ledger's schema and never in `public`.
#
# `sslmode` matters here for the same reason it matters in api.env: every
# example connection string in this repository ends in sslmode=disable, so the
# value most likely to be pasted into a deployment is the one that must never
# be there. Unlike the api, Blnk does not refuse to start on a cleartext URL —
# nothing enforces this but the person editing the file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLNK_SERVER_SECRET_KEY — the credential the ledger accepts.
#
# THIS KEY DOES NOTHING ON ITS OWN. An earlier revision of this file said
# setting it "makes the ledger demand a credential". It does not. Blnk checks
# credentials only when `BLNK_SERVER_SECURE` is true, and in v0.15.2 the
# middleware short-circuits before it ever looks at a key:
#
#   api/middleware/auth.go, Authenticate():
#       if err == nil && conf != nil && !conf.Server.Secure {
#           // Skip authentication when secure mode is disabled
#           c.Next()
#           return
#       }
#
# `Server.Secure` is never defaulted, so it is false unless set, and Blnk logs
# "SECURITY: server.secure is false - API authentication is DISABLED" at every
# start. The switch is set to true in
# deploy/hetzner/compose/docker-compose.cashback.yml, alongside the container
# names it belongs with; this file carries only the secret.
#
# Set it, and set the api's BLNK_SECRET_KEY in api.env to the SAME value. Two
# halves of one setting: the ledger accepts this value, the api presents it.
#
# It is not optional on a deployed environment. Every Apivo environment runs
# APP_ENV=prod, and the api REFUSES TO START with cashback enabled and
# BLNK_SECRET_KEY unset (internal/platform/config: requireCashbackComplete) —
# "a ledger reachable without a credential is a ledger anybody on that network
# can post to, and postings are money".
#
# Left empty with secure mode on, the ledger fails CLOSED: every authenticated
# route answers 401 rather than letting the caller through. Loud and wrong is
# the right way round; `/health` still answers, so the container's healthcheck
# keeps working and the failure surfaces at the api instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BLNK_SERVER_SECRET_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything else Blnk exposes has a documented default and is left unset on
# purpose. The ones worth knowing exist, should an incident ask for them:
# transaction batching and locking, queue sharding and retries, reconciliation
# strategy, webhook delivery, and backups. All are `BLNK_`-prefixed and all
# are documented at https://docs.blnkfinance.com/advanced/configuration.
#
# Blnk's own webhook and Typesense search features are NOT used: cashback
# reads its evidence from polled network transactions (ADR-0003) and searches
# nothing through the ledger. Leaving them unconfigured is the intended state,
# not an oversight to be tidied up later.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
